+### GVKF Voxel Grid Management
+GVKF (Gaussian Voxel Kernel Functions) manages voxel grids for surface reconstruction in open scenes [S1].
+- **Step:** Hide Gaussians with opacity less than 0 during each iteration [S1]. Subdivide qualifying voxels using an octree method with a maximum recursion depth of 3 to cap the total number of Gaussians [S1]. Evaluate voxel subdivision and reclamation every 500 iterations [S1].
+- **Parameter:** Initial voxel size for downsampling the Gaussian point cloud is 0.01 [S1]. Number of MLPs shared across all voxel grids in a scene is 4, decoding different Gaussian attributes from corresponding voxels [S1]. Gradient threshold for voxel subdivision is $2 \times 10^{-4}$; voxels with average gradient exceeding this value are subdivided [S1].
+- **Layout:** Feature vector $F$ per voxel has dimension 32, storing relative coordinates for up to 10 Gaussian points per voxel grid [S1].
+- **Pitfall:** Sparse viewpoint coverage areas lead to irregular Gaussian distribution, causing uneven surfaces and geometric inaccuracy [S1].
 
 ## Background
-The rise of 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) [S1, unofficial] created a need to integrate these continuous, point-based representations into traditional voxel-based pipelines used in robotics, simulation, and neural rendering. Early methods focused on simple density estimation, but recent developments emphasize structured voxel grids that preserve geometric and photometric information for downstream tasks like collision detection and 3D convolution [S1, unofficial]. The field has diverged into specialized tools for specific engines (e.g., Omniverse) and general-purpose neural processing backends.
+The concept of Gaussian Voxelization, specifically in the context of Gaussian Voxel Kernel Functions (GVKF), was introduced in the paper "GVKF: Gaussian Voxel Kernel Functions for Highly Efficient Surface Reconstruction in Open Scenes" [S1]. The method addresses the high memory consumption and geometric inaccuracies of standard 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) in open scenes by using a voxel-based implicit representation. It was developed to improve surface reconstruction quality while maintaining efficiency through shared MLPs and adaptive voxel subdivision [S1].
 
